# Server-only (SSOT-03: src/config/env.server.ts)
# The Java service answers every read and write this storefront makes. There is
# no stand-in: with nothing listening here the store renders its error states.
JAVA_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
JAVA_API_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Client-safe (SSOT-03: src/config/env.client.ts). SEC-01: treat as published.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Dev only, and OPTIONAL. Hosts allowed to load Next's /_next/* resources
# cross-origin, so the store can be opened from a phone on the same network.
# Next blocks these by default: without it the page renders but NO client
# JavaScript runs, so links work and search, the bag and checkout do not.
# Comma-separated bare hosts, no scheme or port. Leave unset on a machine that
# only ever serves localhost.
# DEV_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=192.168.1.50

# Architecture §24 Try-On, and OPTIONAL. Read by src/features/try-on, which
# calls the image model from the Next server; neither value reaches the
# browser (SEC-01).
#
# The key is the ONLY thing standing between this checkout and a real
# generation: `imageModelProvider` in src/features/try-on/api/gemini-provider.ts
# posts the customer's photograph and the garment's to Google's
# generativelanguage endpoint. With no key the feature reports itself
# unavailable (§28.5). The try-on button is drawn either way; ProductTryOn.tsx
# explains why it is never hidden.
#
# Generations are metered and billed by the provider, so treat the key as a
# cost as well as a credential. SEC-10: it belongs in .env.local, which is
# gitignored, and in the host's own environment settings — never in committed
# code, and never pasted into a chat transcript.
# TRY_ON_PROVIDER_API_KEY=
# TRY_ON_PROVIDER_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-image
